      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{32}+\\frac{8}{12}]-[\\frac{16}{32}-\\frac{8}{12}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "result": "Correct",
      "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14223760/observations/history-solution-q-136955-7d531dae0a0940c9534a.html",
